{"packet_id":"25fa27b0ca6da834","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A1","A2"],"reason":"A1 restates the question and lists three generic options: no expansion, targeted expansion and diversification. It adds constraints that no source supports. 'No expansion of the existing product line or market reach' contradicts the question it is framing, and the '20,000 euros and a 12-week timeframe' budget is not in the dossier. The packet supplies no budget, so this cost is a proposed assumption, not a fact. A1 also misses a key premise: the dossier excerpt (hach-aquachek-number-one, retrieved in A2) reports existing consumer-facing pool and spa test strips. Under the invented-constraint cap this cannot exceed 1."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A4"],"reason":"The best theory is T2 (A4). It names a benefit (premium pricing from eco-conscious or enthusiast consumers) but asserts the behaviour change speculatively: consumers are 'likely to perceive... may be willing to pay more'. It gives no reason why these consumers would switch. Its value capture relies on marketing 'existing instruments', which conflicts with the idea of a differentiated proposition. No chain runs from behaviour change to value this manufacturer captures."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A3","A4"],"reason":"T1 (no meaningful consumer demand, stay focused) and T2 (niche premium demand) are genuine rival answers to the same question. T1 is essentially a null or status-quo position, and neither theory specifies different products or channels in enough detail to reach rung 3."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A3","A4"],"reason":"The assumptions and predictions are stated but vague. Examples: 'growing demand', 'revenue will remain stable or increase slightly', 'customer satisfaction will improve', 'brand position will strengthen'. None sets an observable threshold or time window that would put the theory in trouble if it failed."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A5","A6"],"reason":"A5 cites hach-veralto-customers as support for T1. That source describes industrial and municipal customers and parent resources, and says nothing about consumer needs or whether existing instruments suffice for consumers. A6 cites the AquaChek 'number one brand' claim as support for T2's eco-conscious premium niche, but the claim concerns an existing pool and spa strip business and says nothing about sustainability-driven willingness to pay. A6 does label it a company assertion, which fits the manufacturer-sourced wording. However, the 'Does not show' limits in A5 and A6 are identical boilerplate, with no statement of date, place or fit to the customer group. The run cites what agrees and misreads relevance."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A7"],"reason":"A7 claims to search against T2 but describes T2 with T1's content ('existing instruments are sufficient'). It returned the same three dossier sources and reports nothing about what they showed for or against either theory. There was no genuine adverse search and no fair report of the outcome."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A8"],"reason":"The survey of 500 consumers is a proposal, and its cost is an assumption. Its decision rule refers to an undefined 'T2 group'. It uses an arbitrary 70% threshold on 'need for additional testing tools', which does not test T2's distinguishing claims about premium willingness to pay or values alignment. Recruiting people 'interested in water quality monitoring' biases the sample toward T2. Stated-preference measures cannot cleanly separate the rivals, and no use case or product concept is specified."},"revision":{"status":"not_applicable","score":null,"event_ids":[],"reason":"Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled or delivered."},"recommendation":{"status":"not_reached","score":null,"event_ids":["A9"],"reason":"A9 shows the investigation stopped before any recommendation because of repeated errors. This counts as zero toward the all-case composite."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"incomplete","event_ids":["A9"],"reason":"The episode ended with no completed recommendation."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A5"],"reason":"An industrial and municipal customer-scope claim is treated as support for consumer sufficiency (T1), which the source does not address."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A6"],"reason":"The AquaChek 'number one brand' manufacturer claim is used as support for eco-conscious premium demand (T2), which the source does not address. It is also a marketing claim treated as evidence of demand."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A8"],"reason":"The decision rule references an undefined group and measures generic 'need' rather than T2's distinguishing premium or values prediction. The biased sample cannot separate T1 from T2."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"The framing invents constraints (a 20,000 euro, 12-week budget; 'no expansion') absent from the dossier. 'No expansion' contradicts the question being investigated."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A7"],"reason":"The challenge step mislabels T2 with T1's content and reports no findings, so it cannot count as a genuine challenge."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"Rejected because the episode is incomplete: it stopped at A9 with no recommendation. It also contains unsupported evidence inferences (A5, A6), a non-discriminating proposed test (A8) and invented framing constraints (A1)."}
